  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM CDT Sat May 9 2026\n\nAn area of light to moderate rain with isolated thunder\nwill continue to move through much of the PAH forecast area (fa)\nthrough the rest of the night, with models indicating the \nactivity lessening overall as it moves east, with the activity \nmoving east of our area by daybreak. The most substantial \nrainfall of a quarter inch or more will be north of Route 13 in \nsouthern Illinois, with lighter amounts farther south and east.\n\nDry conditions can be expected today and tonight, with much\nwarmer temperatures. Highs will reach around 80 degrees today\nunder mostly sunny skies, with seasonable lows tonight in the\nmiddle to upper 50s.  \n\nModels bring a weak cold front through our area late Saturday\nnight, followed by a weak upper level wave Sunday. Any\nconvection will be with the upper wave. With better moisture to\nour south, shower and thunderstorm chances (30-40%) will be \nfocused across southern portions of southeast Missouri and west\nKentucky from late Sunday morning into Sunday night. Rainfall \namounts will be less than a tenth of an inch.\n\nCooler air will filter in late Sunday into Monday. We will see a\nbroad temperature gradient Sunday, with highs ranging from the \nupper 60s northwest to around 80 degrees southeast, then by \nMonday, readings will be below normal across the entire PAH fa \nin the upper 60s to lower 70s. \n\nTuesday night, models show a surface low moving through the\nGreat Lakes region, dragging a cold front through our area. \nMoisture will really be lacking. The model blend produces 30-40%\nchances of showers and storms mainly across southern Illinois, \nsouthwest Indiana, northwest Kentucky and the Pennyrile region, \nbut rainfall amounts are meager, showing a couple of hundredths \nat best. Beyond Tuesday night, there are currently no \nindications of any worthwhile rain chances through Friday.\n\nTemperatures for Tuesday through the end of the week will\nmoderate back to near to a little above normal, with the warmest\nconditions on Friday as a return of south winds helps push \ntemperatures into the 80s.",
